\chapter{Expression Templates: Lazy Trees and Fused Evaluation}
\label{ch:expression-templates}

\section{The Problem: Eager Arithmetic and the Temporaries It Costs}

Write \code{a + b * c} where \code{a}, \code{b} and \code{c} are vectors of
one million doubles. A conventional \cpp{} evaluation computes \code{b * c}
into a fresh heap allocation, computes \code{a + (that)} into a second fresh
heap allocation, and hands the second one back. Two allocations, two full
passes over a million elements, and one of the two results thrown away before
the caller ever sees it. For a numeric DSL this is not a micro-optimisation;
it is the difference between a language that a user can afford to write
expressions in and one they cannot.

The classic remedy is the \emph{expression template}: instead of computing a
subexpression, build a small tree that records what was asked for, and compute
the whole thing in one pass when the value is finally needed. This is the
technique behind \code{std::valarray}, behind Eigen's expression templates,
and behind every numeric library that promises "no temporaries". It is
notoriously hard to implement, because the operator overloads must return
something that behaves like a value in every context except the one that
matters, and because the deferred tree must be able to hold heterogeneous
operand types without erasing them.

\DSLtk{} provides a compact implementation of this idea. The
\code{ExprTemplates} feature installs arithmetic operators on your DSL type
that return lazy tree nodes rather than results, and every tree node can
evaluate itself into a value. What the shipped implementation does
\emph{not} do is fuse element-wise array operations the way Eigen does. The
evaluation is recursive: it evaluates operand values and invokes operations,
which can create intermediate objects. That distinction is the subject of this chapter,
and it is worth understanding precisely, because the header's own documentation
suggests otherwise.

\section{A First Worked Example: Scalar Arithmetic}

The shipped example \code{DSLtk/examples/12-expression-templates.cpp} is the
smallest possible demonstration of the facility. It defines a leaf type with a
single double and an \code{expr_value()} accessor, and it builds an expression
without evaluating it.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={The shipped expression-template example, reproduced verbatim from \code{DSLtk/examples/12-expression-templates.cpp}. The leaf type supplies \code{expr_value()}; the mixin supplies the operators; \code{eval()} produces the number.},label={lst:ch17-scalar}]
#include "DSLtk.hpp"
#include <iostream>

struct Scalar : dsl::DSL<Scalar, dsl::ExprTemplates> {
  double v{};
  Scalar() = default;
  explicit Scalar(double x) : v(x) {}
  double expr_value() const { return v; }
};

int main() {
  Scalar a{2}, b{3}, c{4};
  auto expr = a + b * c - a;
  std::cout << expr.eval() << "\n";
}
\end{lstlisting}

The output is \code{12}. The important thing to notice is not the number but
the type of \code{expr}. Because \code{a + b * c - a} involves three
operator applications and no arithmetic at all, \code{expr} is a
\code{dsl::BinExpr} node, and the multiplication is a nested
\code{dsl::BinExpr} node inside it. No double has been added, multiplied or
subtracted. The \code{eval()} call at the end of \code{main} is the only
place where arithmetic happens, and it happens in a single recursive descent
through the tree.

\section{A Second Worked Example: Element-Wise Arithmetic}

The scalar example is honest but not very interesting, because a scalar
expression has nothing to fuse. The interesting case is an array-valued leaf,
and it requires one piece of machinery that the header does not provide: the
operation tags must know how to combine two arrays.

The mechanism is this. \code{dsl::BinExpr} does not evaluate anything
itself; at the bottom of its \code{eval()} it calls the operation tag with
whatever the two operands evaluated to (\code{DSLtk.hpp}, line 6981). The tag
used for \code{+} is \code{std::plus<>}, and \code{std::plus<>::operator()}
is defined as \code{std::forward<T>(t) + std::forward<U>(u)}. So the tag
does not know anything about arrays; it merely performs a \code{+}. If the
operand type has an element-wise \code{+}, the tag gets element-wise
behaviour for free.

That is the whole trick. The user's array type supplies the arithmetic, and
the expression machinery supplies the deferral.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={Element-wise expression templates. \code{Vec} is an ordinary user-defined array type whose arithmetic operators are element-wise; \code{VecDSL} is the leaf that carries a \code{Vec} and exposes it through \code{expr_value()}. The counter \code{probes} shows that nothing is computed until \code{eval()} is called.},label={lst:ch17-vec}]
#include "DSLtk.hpp"
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

// An ordinary array type whose arithmetic is element-wise.
struct Vec {
  std::vector<double> v;

  std::size_t size() const noexcept { return v.size(); }
  double operator[](std::size_t i) const noexcept { return v[i]; }

  Vec operator+(const Vec &o) const {
    Vec r; r.v.resize(v.size());
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < v.size(); ++i) r.v[i] = v[i] + o.v[i];
    return r;
  }
  Vec operator-(const Vec &o) const {
    Vec r; r.v.resize(v.size());
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < v.size(); ++i) r.v[i] = v[i] - o.v[i];
    return r;
  }
  Vec operator*(const Vec &o) const {
    Vec r; r.v.resize(v.size());
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < v.size(); ++i) r.v[i] = v[i] * o.v[i];
    return r;
  }
  Vec operator/(const Vec &o) const {
    Vec r; r.v.resize(v.size());
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < v.size(); ++i) r.v[i] = v[i] / o.v[i];
    return r;
  }
  Vec operator*(double s) const {
    Vec r; r.v.resize(v.size());
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < v.size(); ++i) r.v[i] = v[i] * s;
    return r;
  }
  Vec operator-() const {
    Vec r; r.v.resize(v.size());
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < v.size(); ++i) r.v[i] = -v[i];
    return r;
  }
};

// The leaf: carries the array and hands it to the evaluator.
int probes = 0;

struct VecDSL : dsl::DSL<VecDSL, dsl::ExprTemplates> {
  Vec data;
  explicit VecDSL(Vec d) : data(std::move(d)) {}
  Vec expr_value() const { ++probes; return data; }
};

int main() {
  VecDSL a{Vec{{1.0, 2.0, 3.0}}};
  VecDSL b{Vec{{10.0, 20.0, 30.0}}};

  auto expr = a + b * 2.0;      // lazy: nothing computed yet
  std::cout << "probes after building the expression: " << probes << "\n";

  Vec r = expr.eval();
  std::cout << "probes after eval(): " << probes << "\n";

  std::cout << "a + b * 2 = ";
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < r.size(); ++i)
    std::cout << (i ? ", " : "") << r[i];
  std::cout << "\n";

  Vec neg = (-a).eval();
  std::cout << "-a        = ";
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < neg.size(); ++i)
    std::cout << (i ? ", " : "") << neg[i];
  std::cout << "\n";
}
\end{lstlisting}

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcmd,caption={Output of Listing~\ref{lst:ch17-vec}. The probe counter shows that the two leaves are read exactly once each, at \code{eval()} time and not before.},label={lst:ch17-vec-out}]
probes after building the expression: 0
probes after eval(): 2
a + b * 2 = 21, 42, 63
-a        = -1, -2, -3
\end{lstlisting}

Two details of this listing deserve emphasis. First, \code{Vec} must be a
user-defined type. The standard \code{std::vector<double>} will not work,
because the operation tag would then be asked to add two vectors, and there
is no \code{operator+} for two \code{std::vector<float>}s. The offending
call is \code{std::plus<>{}(vector, vector)}, and the compiler rejects it
outright. Second, the leaf type must provide \code{expr_value()}. The
\code{ExprTemplates} mixin documents this requirement but does not enforce
it; a leaf without \code{expr_value()} and without \code{eval()} is silently
treated as its own value, which is the failure mode discussed in
Section~\ref{sec:ch17-headerexample}.

The multiplication creates an intermediate \code{Vec}, and the addition
creates another result vector. The value-returning \code{expr_value()}
also copies its stored vector. These objects can coexist during evaluation.
The example demonstrates deferred whole-value operations, not allocation-free
array fusion. A fused implementation would need an indexed evaluation
protocol such as the separate mechanism supplied by the D port.

\section{The Data Model}

The entire facility consists of three types and one feature tag. All of them
live in namespace \code{dsl} between lines 6943 and 7129 of the header.

\code{dsl::ExprTerminal} (line 6950) is declared as an empty struct. It is
described in its own comment as a "tag type indicating a terminal (leaf) in an
expression template tree". It is not a template, it has no members, and it is
referenced nowhere else in the header: a search of the whole file finds the
declaration at line 6950 and no other occurrence. It is therefore dead code
in the shipped implementation -- a placeholder for a concept that the actual
design expresses differently, namely through the \code{requires} expressions
in the dispatch helper. The D port carries the same empty declaration at line
380 of \code{DSLtk.d}, with the same property.

\code{dsl::BinExpr<Op, L, R>} (lines 6963--7002) is a binary tree node. It
has exactly two data members, \code{L lhs} and \code{R rhs}, both stored
\emph{by value}. Storing by value is what makes the tree a self-contained
value type: a \code{BinExpr} can be copied, moved, stored in a container and
returned from a function without any lifetime coupling to the objects that
were combined to build it. The constructor is
\code{constexpr BinExpr(L l, R r)}, and it moves its arguments into place.

\code{dsl::UnaryExpr<Op, E>} (lines 7010--7032) is the corresponding unary
node. It has a single member, \code{E operand}, also stored by value, and an
\code{explicit} constructor, so that a unary node cannot be confused with a
binary one.

The third type is the feature tag \code{dsl::ExprTemplates} (lines
7056--7099). It has no data; it contributes a nested \code{Mixin<Derived>}
template that the \code{dsl::DSL} base inherits from. Because the mixin's
operators are \emph{hidden friends} -- declared inside the class template and
found by argument-dependent lookup -- they are only visible when at least one
operand is of the \code{Derived} type. That is the whole mechanism by which
the feature attaches operators to your DSL type without polluting the global
namespace or interfering with unrelated overloads.

