Table~\ref{tab:ch17-operators} is the complete operator surface. The absence
of the remainder and assignment operators is not an oversight; it is a
consequence of the design. Because the operators return a tree node rather
than a value, \code{a += b} would have to mean "rebuild the tree in place",
which requires the node to be mutable and to own its leaves. The shipped
implementation chooses immutability and value semantics instead, and it is a
reasonable trade: the expression becomes an ordinary value that can be stored,
passed and compared by structure rather than by identity.

The four free functions at lines 7103--7129 are worth a second look, because
their existence is what makes the operator set compose. The hidden friends
require their \emph{left} operand to be \code{Derived}. Without the free
functions, \code{a + b * c} would work (the left operand of \code{+} is
\code{a}, a \code{Derived}) but \code{a + b * c + d} would not, because the
left operand of the second \code{+} would be a \code{BinExpr}. The free
functions close that gap by accepting a \code{BinExpr} on the left and
producing a \code{BinExpr} whose left member is that \code{BinExpr}. The
resulting type nests to the left, which is the mirror image of the way the
operators were written.

\section{The Evaluation Algorithm}

Evaluation of a tree is a single recursive descent. \code{BinExpr::eval()}
(lines 6976--6982) is three lines long:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={The evaluation of a binary node, transcribed from \code{DSLtk.hpp} lines 6976--6982. The private helper \code{eval_operand} is transcribed in Algorithm~\ref{alg:ch17-dispatch}.},label={lst:ch17-eval}]
  constexpr auto
  eval () const
  {
    auto l = eval_operand (lhs);
    auto r = eval_operand (rhs);
    return Op{}(l, r);
  }
\end{lstlisting}

The interesting part is \code{eval_operand} (lines 6985--7001), the private
static helper that decides what an operand \emph{is}. It performs a
three-way dispatch, decided at compile time by \code{if constexpr}
expressions on \code{requires} clauses.

\begin{pseudocode}[Three-way operand dispatch, from \code{DSLtk.hpp} lines 6985--7001]
\textbf{function} eval\_operand (operand)\\
\quad \textbf{if} \code{operand.eval()} is well-formed then\\
\qquad \textbf{return} \code{operand.eval()}\\
\quad \textbf{else if} \code{operand.expr_value()} is well-formed then\\
\qquad \textbf{return} \code{operand.expr_value()}\\
\quad \textbf{else}\\
\qquad \textbf{return} \code{operand} itself
\label{alg:ch17-dispatch}
\end{pseudocode}

Algorithm~\ref{alg:ch17-dispatch} is a compile-time decision, not a runtime
one. The \code{requires} expression is a substitution-failure context, so if
the operand type has a callable \code{eval()} member the first branch is
selected and the other two are not even instantiated. The order matters: a
node that has \emph{both} \code{eval()} and \code{expr_value()} -- which is
the case for a nested \code{BinExpr}, since \code{BinExpr} has \code{eval()}
but no \code{expr_value()} -- is resolved by \code{eval()}. A leaf that
provides \code{expr_value()} is resolved by the second branch. A leaf that
provides neither is returned unchanged, which is what makes plain scalars
usable as right-hand operands: \code{b * 2.0} evaluates its right operand
to the double \code{2.0}.

The unary node inlines exactly the same three-way dispatch (lines
7019--7030) rather than calling the binary helper, because the two cases
differ in arity and inlining avoids a spurious copy of the operand.

The evaluation order is left to right: \code{l} is computed before \code{r},
and both are computed before the operation is applied. That ordering is
observable if the leaf types have side effects, and it is worth knowing which
order you get rather than discovering it.

Figure~\ref{fig:ch17-tree} shows the tree for \code{a + b * 2.0} before and
after evaluation.

