\section{What ``Fusion'' Means Here, and What It Does Not}

It is tempting to read \code{eval()} as performing element-wise fusion, in
the sense that Eigen performs it: the whole expression \code{a + b * 2.0} is
evaluated into a single output buffer in one linear pass, with no
intermediate array ever materialised. The shipped \cpp{} implementation does
\emph{not} do this, and the distinction is not cosmetic.

In the \cpp{} header, fusion is purely \emph{structural}. The tree is walked
once, in post-order, and each node's result is produced by applying its
operation tag to the two already-evaluated children. Whether that avoids
temporaries depends entirely on what the operation tag does. With
\code{std::multiplies<>} applied to two \code{Vec} values, a \code{Vec} is
produced; with a tag that wrote into a caller-supplied output buffer, no
intermediate would be needed. The header offers no such tag, no output
parameter on \code{eval()}, and no element-indexed evaluation hook. There is
no \code{eval_at(index)} in the \cpp{} port at all.

What the \cpp{} port \emph{does} guarantee is that the deferred expression is
not evaluated eagerly and that the number of tree nodes is proportional to the
number of operators written, not to the number of elements. For a leaf that
returns a scalar this is exactly right: \code{a + b * c - a} performs three
arithmetic operations, once each, when \code{eval()} is called. For a leaf
that returns an array, the operation tags perform their own element-wise
loops, and the number of passes over the data is the number of operators in
the expression. That is still better than the eager baseline in one respect
--- the tree is built once and can be evaluated many times --- but it is not
the single-pass fusion that the word ``expression template'' usually implies.

To obtain true element-wise fusion in \cpp{} with this header, the user must
supply operation tags that fuse. Writing a bespoke tag that accumulates into
an output buffer, and a leaf whose \code{expr_value()} exposes its elements
one at a time, is possible but is not supported by any API in the header: the
dispatch helper in Algorithm~\ref{alg:ch17-dispatch} knows only about whole
operands.

\section{The Header's Own Example Does Not Work}
\label{sec:ch17-headerexample}

The quick-start comment at the top of \code{DSLtk.hpp} contains, at lines
308--316, an "Example C --- VecDSL (ExprTemplates)" that does not compile and
does not evaluate. The example reads:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={The header's Example C, transcribed from \code{DSLtk.hpp} lines 308--316. It is reproduced here because it does not work, and the reason is instructive.},label={lst:ch17-badexample}]
struct VecDSL : dsl::DSL<VecDSL, dsl::ExprTemplates> {
    std::vector<float> data;
};
// Usage:
VecDSL a{{1,2,3}}, b{{4,5,6}};
auto expr   = a + b;          // lazy
auto result = expr.eval();    // {5, 7, 9}
\end{lstlisting}

There are two independent problems with it.

The first is trivial and mechanical. \code{VecDSL} is an aggregate, and so
is its base,
\code{dsl::DSL<VecDSL, dsl::ExprTemplates>} -- itself an aggregate whose own
base is the feature mixin. Aggregate initialisation of \code{VecDSL}
therefore requires a braced initialiser for the mixin base, so the printed
\code{VecDSL a{{1,2,3}}} is rejected: the compiler asks for the mixin to be
brace-enclosed. The working form is \code{VecDSL a{{}, {1,2,3}}}, or a
user-declared constructor, as in Listing~\ref{lst:ch17-vec}.

The second problem is the interesting one, and it is a genuine design
hazard in the feature. \code{VecDSL} has no \code{expr_value()} and no
\code{eval()}, so the third branch of Algorithm~\ref{alg:ch17-dispatch}
applies: the operand is returned as itself. \code{eval()} therefore holds two
\code{VecDSL} values and calls the operation tag on them. But \code{l + r} on
two \code{VecDSL} values resolves to the very hidden friend \code{operator+}
that the mixin installed, which builds \emph{another} \code{BinExpr}. The
evaluation therefore re-enters the operator machinery instead of reaching
the payload, and \code{expr.eval()} returns a fresh lazy tree rather than a
vector. The type it returns is
\code{dsl::BinExpr<std::plus<>, VecDSL, VecDSL>}, and evaluating that
result produces the same type again: the evaluation is a fixed point that
never terminates in a value. Compiling the example as printed and asking for
the type of \code{expr.eval()} confirms this exactly.

The obvious repair -- adding an \code{expr_value()} that returns the
vector -- does not help either. The operation tag would then be asked to add
two vectors, which is ill-formed, because there is no \code{operator+} for
two \code{std::vector<float>}s; the compiler reports precisely that. This is
the same lesson that Listing~\ref{lst:ch17-vec} teaches: the leaf's value
type must supply the arithmetic. The header's example supplies neither the
accessor nor the arithmetic, and it consequently produces neither a number
nor a vector.

\section{Contrast with the D Port}

The D implementation in \code{DSLtk.d} does have element-wise fusion, and the
difference is instructive enough to be worth reading side by side. Its
expression trees live at lines 379--474, and they carry three pieces of
machinery that have no counterpart in \cpp{}.

First, the D \code{BinExpr} is parameterised by an operation \emph{string},
not by a tag type. Its declaration is given in
Listing~\ref{lst:ch17-dbin}. The operation is mixed into the generated code
with a D \code{mixin} expression, which is how D code-generates a call to an
operator that is determined at compile time.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslsexpr,caption={The D binary expression node, transcribed from \code{DSLtk.d} lines 428--432. The operation is a compile-time string, mixed into the generated expression.},label={lst:ch17-dbin}]
struct BinExpr(string op, L, R) if (arithmetic!op)
{
    L lhs;
    R rhs;
    mixin ExpressionOperators;
\end{lstlisting}

Second, D has the notion of an \emph{indexed} operand, computed by the
private template \code{indexed!T} at line 415. An operand is indexed if it
has an \code{eval_at} member, if it is a D array, or if its
\code{expr_value()} returns an array. The private helper \code{operandAt}
(line 417) fetches element \code{i} of an operand, and \code{operandLength}
(line 423) determines the length of the result.

Third, and decisively, when either operand is indexed the D \code{BinExpr}
grows an \code{eval_at} member and an \code{eval()} that allocates one
result array and fills it element by element. The body of the former is
reproduced in Listing~\ref{lst:ch17-devat}; the latter loops over the
indices, calling \code{eval_at} once per element (lines 447--453). That is
exactly the element-wise fusion the \cpp{} port lacks: one output buffer, one
linear pass, and a single length check that throws when the operands
disagree (line 440).

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslsexpr,caption={The D element-wise evaluation hook, transcribed from \code{DSLtk.d} lines 446--453. The expression is assembled by the D \code{mixin} template, so the operation is spliced into the generated code at compile time.},label={lst:ch17-devat}]
        auto eval_at(size_t i) { return mixin("operandAt(lhs, i) " ~ op ~ " operandAt(rhs, i)"); }
        auto eval()
        {
            alias T = typeof(eval_at(0));
            auto result = new T[length()];
            foreach (i; 0 .. result.length) result[i] = eval_at(i);
            return result;
        }
\end{lstlisting}

So the same conceptual facility exists in both ports, but the \cpp{} version
is a deferral mechanism with a structural single-pass evaluation, while the D
version is a deferral mechanism with genuine element-wise fusion. When
reading the older \DSLtk{} manual, it is the D behaviour that is usually
described; the \cpp{} header is authoritative.

\section{Edge Cases and Limits}

The following list is the honest summary of what this facility does not do.

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Only five arithmetic operators exist.} There is no remainder
operator, no comparison, no assignment operator, and no stream insertion.
A program that wants \code{a < b} on DSL values must define that operator
itself, and because the hidden friends are found by argument-dependent
lookup, a user-defined \code{operator<} on the \code{Derived} type will
coexist with them without ambiguity.

\item \textbf{There is no element-wise fusion in the \cpp{} port.} As
Section~\ref{sec:ch17-headerexample} explains, \code{eval()} walks the tree
once but each node applies its operation tag to whole operands. Eliminating
intermediate arrays requires user-supplied operation tags and a
user-supplied element-access protocol; the header provides neither.

\item \textbf{The dispatch order is fixed.} \code{eval()} is tried before
\code{expr_value()}. A leaf type that defines both will have its
\code{eval()} used, which is usually what one wants but is never stated in
the mixin's documentation.

\item \textbf{Leaf types without an accessor silently become their own
value.} The third branch of Algorithm~\ref{alg:ch17-dispatch} means that a
leaf with no \code{eval()} and no \code{expr_value()} is passed straight to
the operation tag. If the tag can accept the leaf type -- as \code{std::plus<>}
can, when the leaf has an \code{operator+} of its own -- the expression
evaluates to something surprising. If the tag cannot accept it, the error
appears far from its cause, at the point of the \code{eval()} call rather than
at the point of the leaf's definition.

\item \textbf{Operands are stored by value.} A \code{BinExpr} copies its
leaves. For a leaf holding a large array this copy happens when the tree is
built, not when it is evaluated, which means the deferral does not extend to
the cost of acquiring the operands. A leaf that holds a handle to shared
immutable data avoids the copy; a leaf that holds an array by value does not.

\item \textbf{Trees are not comparable.} There is no \code{operator==} for
\code{BinExpr} or \code{UnaryExpr}, so two structurally identical trees are
unequal in the \cpp{} sense. A tree can of course be compared by evaluating
both sides and comparing the results.

\item \textbf{There is no reuse, no partial evaluation and no caching.}
Calling \code{eval()} twice evaluates the tree twice. There is no notion of
a partially evaluated tree, of constant folding, or of a common
subexpression shared between two trees.

\item \textbf{Only the left-hand \code{BinExpr} case is covered.} The free
operators accept a \code{BinExpr} on the left, so \code{(a + b) * c}
compiles. A \code{UnaryExpr} on the left of a binary operator does not: there
is no free \code{operator+} for a \code{UnaryExpr} on the left. Writing
\code{-(a + b) * c} is therefore ill-formed, whereas \code{(a + b) * c} is
fine.

\item \textbf{\code{eval()} is \code{constexpr} but rarely usable as such.}
The declaration is \code{constexpr}, so a tree over literal types can be
evaluated at compile time. In practice the C++20 standard library offers
little that is usable inside a constant expression, and the leaf types of
interest are not literal types.
\end{itemize}

\section{Closing}

The \code{ExprTemplates} feature gives a DSL author arithmetic operators that
build a value rather than perform a computation, and gives them a single
\code{eval()} that walks the resulting tree once. Its machinery is small:
two node templates, a three-way compile-time dispatch, a set of hidden
friends, and four free functions that let a tree node appear on the left of
a further operation. The interesting design decisions are all in the
negative space: no other operators, no element-wise fusion in the \cpp{} port,
and a fallback branch that turns a missing accessor into a silently wrong
answer rather than a compile error.

The next chapter turns to a facility with the opposite character. Where
expression templates defer a computation that the programmer has already
written down, custom literals let a programmer write down a \emph{surface
form} -- \code{"3.5_km"} -- that the library turns into a number at run
time.
